WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:48.629
[A radiologist places X-rays on a screen to
be read.]

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:54.809
[Narrator:] The management of cancer involves
the services of several disciplines.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:58.289
Utilizing the judgment and skills of the surgeon.

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:01.019
The pathologist.

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:03.670
The internist.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.179
And the radiation therapist.

00:01:07.179 --> 00:01:13.110
At the very outset, it is important to determine
which modality of treatment, or combination,

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.069
will most likely benefit the patient.

00:01:16.069 --> 00:01:23.069
And whether treatment is to be given with
intent to cure, or for palliation.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:32.660
Radiation doses and treatment schedules vary
greatly depending upon therapeutic aims.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.970
And the radiation therapist, like the surgeon
and chemotherapist,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:43.580
is guided by the classic dictum, "First do no harm".

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:48.880
The objective: to deliver a cancerocidal dose
to the tumor

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.980
with the least possible damage to the surrounding
tissues.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:02.450
In outlining a treatment plan, the therapist
considers many factors.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.749
The general status of the patient.

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:07.979
The type and location of the tumor.

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:13.819
Its volume. Its expected response to radiation.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:16.879
These and other physical and biological factors
will govern the selection 

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.540
of the appropriate type of radiation.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:32.120
Isodose charts showing the penetration of
the beam for a given energy,

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:39.120
will help verify the suitability of the radiation
for the particular location of the tumor.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.720
Radiation therapy uses a variety of equipment,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.320
providing a wide spectrum of wavelengths and penetration characteristics.

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:56.369
This low voltage, 100 kilovolt unit is suitable for treating superficial, thin lesions,

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.920
such as this basal cell carcinoma.

00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:17.009
The 100 kilovolt unit produces soft x-rays
of low penetration,

00:03:17.009 --> 00:03:20.729
 which deposit most of their energy in the first few centimeters of tissue.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:28.500
Generators in the 250 to 300 kilovolt range
produce x-rays of somewhat greater penetration,

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:37.970
useful for treating superficial thick lesions;
as for example, this tumor of the carotid gland.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:49.660
Megavolt -- million volt equipment -- produces still harder, more penetrating radiation.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:57.670
A two million volt machine, such as this resonant transformer, is suitable for treating deeper lesions.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:04.760
Here, used to irradiate paraaortic lymph node metastases, in a patient with a testicular carcinoma.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:15.940
Six million volt x-rays are produced by this
linear accelerator.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:23.810
With a betatron, 35 or even 45 million electron
volt x-rays can be obtained.

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:31.710
The penetration achieved with these high energies
permits treatment at depths beyond the effective

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:36.810
reach of lower energy beams as seen in this
comparison.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:44.340
As noted earlier, the energy from a low-voltage,
100 KEV unit is quickly absorbed.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:48.509
And does not penetrate much beyond the superficial
tissues.

00:04:48.509 --> 00:04:55.509
With approximately eight percent of the maximum
dose measurable at a depth of 10 cm.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:03.820
Approximately 35 percent of the maximum dose
reaches this level with 250 KEV radiation.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:10.150
The two million volt generator delivers approximately
50 percent of its maximum dose at this depth.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:14.940
The six million volt linear accelerator delivers
70 percent,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:21.940
and the 35 MEV betatron delivers a full 85
to 90 percent of its dose maximum at 10 centimeters.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:33.020
Isodose charts used by the therapist in planning
treatment show the dose distribution in percentages

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:40.030
of the maximum dose for a given energy at
various depths below the skin's surface.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:50.120
Each chart identifies the modality, the source
skin distance, and the field size.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.150
The penetration of these high energy beams permits effective irradiation of deep-seated tumors.

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:10.040
In addition, at higher energies, the edges
of the beam are sharp because these highly

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.020
energetic x-rays are not significantly scattered
by the irradiated tissues,

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.330
either backward or laterally,

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:25.330
as is the case with low-energy radiation where
there is significant scatter outside the field.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:30.490
The more sharply defined edges of the high
energy beam confine the radiation

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.180
 more precisely to the treated area.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.620
Another advantage of high-energy radiation
is that the maximum dose is not reached at

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:47.150
the skin's surface, but at varying depths
beneath it...

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:54.030
so that skin reactions are not a limiting
factor.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:59.180
High energy ionizing radiation in the megavolt
range can also be obtained with relatively

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:06.180
simple equipment using radioactive isotopes
such as Cobalt-60.

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:09.320
The Cobalt-60 unit emits gamma radiations,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:16.020
which are similar in penetration to x-rays
produced by a three million volt generator.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.860
The gamma radiation from isotopes is indistinguishable

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.400
from electrically-produced x-rays of the same energy.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:31.720
Of all high energy installations in use today,
more than 80 percent are Cobalt-60 units.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:36.860
Here, used to treat the patient with Hodgkins Disease.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:47.700
The quantity of emitted radiation is expressed in roentgens, a unit of measure of ionization in air.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:56.120
However, the amount of energy absorbed in
tissue is measured in rads, the unit of radiation-absorbed does.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:04.760
One rad equalling 100 ergs of energy absorbed per gram of tissue.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:12.120
Absorbed dose, however, varies with tissue
composition and radiation energy.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:18.560
As illustrated in this x-ray film, at low
energies -- 250 KEV or less -- there is a

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.050
significant difference in absorption between
soft tissue and bone.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:29.050
because of the greater absorption of energy
by the denser, skeletal tissues.

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:35.540
At higher energies, this absorption differential does not exist, and the amount of energy

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:39.259
absorbed by bone and soft tissue is about equal.

00:08:39.259 --> 00:08:43.990
This allows treatment to cancerocidal doses
in the vicinity of bone without producing

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:48.110
bone damage because of increased absorption.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:56.500
This side-by-side comparison shows the difference
in absorption at low and high energies.

00:08:59.959 --> 00:09:04.990
The effects of radiation are initiated by
the interaction of the x-rays or gamma rays

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:09.360
with the orbital electrons of the tissue atoms.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:15.200
The displaced electrons and scattered photons
cause increasingly large numbers of ionizations

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.160
as they penetrate the tissue.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:28.770
The released electrons act as chemically reactive
reducing substances, forming free radicals

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:34.430
and producing chemical damage, including damage
to DNA.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.990
Damage to DNA may be reparable, or may result
in cell death, 

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.870
clinically observable as tumor shrinkage.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:52.240
The initial manifestation of cell death often
occurs at the time of mitosis.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:58.390
This cell, which has previously been irradiated,
is about to divide.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.600
At first it appears normal.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:07.600
However, as cell division proceeds, the chromosomes
tend to clump together, and a chromosome bridge

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:12.480
can be seen to connect the two potential daughter
cells.

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:15.650
This abortive division is one of a number
of manifestations

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.580
 of lethal cell damage following irradiation.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:24.310
In general, the sensitivity of a cell to the
effects of radiation 

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.261
seems largely related to its mitotic activity.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:33.520
Cells which are mitotically most active are
usually the most radiosensitive,

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:41.420
illustrated here by this poorly differentiated,
squamous cell carcinoma of the nasal pharynx.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:49.570
Among the most radiosensitive tumors are the
malignant lymphomas and leukemias.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:53.230
Undifferentiated, squamous cell carcinomas 

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:57.920
and germinal tumors such as seminomas and dysgerminomas.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:03.860
Moderately radiosensitive are the well-differentiated squamous cell carcinomas 

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:06.280
of the skin and mucus membranes,

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:11.760
adenocarcinomas, and some sarcomas.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:17.000
Relatively radio-resistant tumors include
osteogenic sarcomas,

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:21.180
 malignant melanomas, and some gliomas.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:28.620
Radiosensitivity of a tumor however, is not
necessarily related to curability,

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:35.430
since tumors with a high degree of radiosensitivity
are often those which also show marked anaplasia,

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:40.529
aggressive growth, and wide dissemination.

00:11:40.529 --> 00:11:44.809
In fact, the chances for cure of a cancer
by irradiation depend mainly 

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:50.720
on its location, its size and accessibility, its biological behavior,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:56.140
and probably on immunological factors.

00:11:56.149 --> 00:11:59.849
The general condition of the patient is of
considerable importance,

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:04.280
 and can significantly influence the patient's tolerance to irradiation.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:12.440
Cancer of the cervix is a classic example
in which radiation therapy is given with the intent to cure.

00:12:14.459 --> 00:12:19.230
With irradiation as the principal treatment
modality, recent five-year survival rates

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:25.410
free of disease were reported by a major institution as follows.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:32.030
[Graph titled 5 Year Survival Rates/Cancer of the Cervix is shown.]

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:38.140
This patient was initially examined by her
referring gynecologist, 

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:41.060
who biopsied a lesion on the cervix.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:50.320
Examination of the biopsy specimen showed a moderately differentiated, squamous cell carcinoma.

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:58.420
And she was referred to the radiation therapist for consultation.

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:04.670
The cystoscopic examination and intravenous
urogram were within normal limits.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:09.010
Blood count and chemistries were also normal.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:15.400
Vaginal examination revealed a normal-sized, slightly anteflexed uterus

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:20.140
 with a tumor involving the external cervical os

00:13:20.149 --> 00:13:24.849
and extending into the left fornix.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:33.340
On rectal/vaginal examination, the lesion
is felt to involve the medial aspect of the

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:37.250
left parametrium without reaching the pelvic
wall,

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:41.210
classifying this as a stage 2B tumor.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:45.750
In order to plan the treatment, certain measurements are needed. 

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:50.360
The anterior/posterior diameter is measured

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:56.400
and recorded on plotting paper.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:04.880
The contour of the pelvis is then obtained
with lead tape,

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:11.099
and is also traced...

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:19.160
and superimposed on the appropriate isodose chart. 

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:23.630
Treatment will be given through two opposed fields.

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:30.710
The dose contribution of the anterior field
is plotted.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:37.760
And then that of the posterior.

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:46.820
This will result in a relatively homogenous
dose distribution in the volume to be treated,

00:14:46.829 --> 00:14:51.549
avoiding regions of overdosage or underdosage.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:59.220
Compounding of the doses contributed by each
field results in these summation curves.

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:11.960
The limits of the treatment field are outlined
on the skin and entered in the patient's treatment chart.

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:20.820
She will receive daily treatments of 200 rads

00:15:20.829 --> 00:15:24.829
through both the anterior and posterior fields,

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:28.800
until a tumor dose of about 4,000 rads has been reached,

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:36.360
well within the tolerance of the normal pelvic
tissue.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:44.440
Fractionating the total dose into small increments
over a protracted period results in greater

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:51.269
dose tolerance and favors recovery of the
noncancerous tissues.

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:56.900
The field size is optically simulated,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:16:02.690
using a beam collimator, which is adjusted
to conform with a treatment field.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:10.220
And the treatment is started. 

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:14.820
If there are no interruptions in the treatment schedule,

00:16:14.829 --> 00:16:20.269
the goal of 4,000 rads will be obtained in
approximately four weeks.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:29.160
On the last day of cobalt therapy, the left
parametrium has returned to almost normal consistency.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:37.779
And the lesion at the cervical os is now visible as a small, whitish area of neurotic tumor tissue.

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:48.280
Treatment will be completed with intravaginal and intrauterine radium therapy.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:53.150
This permits delivery of a very high dose
to the central, most resistant part of the lesion.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:17:00.399
And simultaneously increases the dose to the
lateral, pelvic tissues.

00:17:00.399 --> 00:17:05.899
A tumor dose of the level attained by this
method cannot be reached by external radiation alone.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:16.280
After cervical dilatation, a tandem is inserted into the uterine canal.

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:24.580
Followed by colpostats placed into the lateral
fornices.

00:17:37.649 --> 00:17:43.100
The position of the radium-containing applicators
is verified radiographically.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:50.100
These films permit calculation of the exact
dose delivered to the tumor and adjacent structures.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:18:00.280
Examination after completion of the full course
of treatment shows the healed cervix.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:09.420
Radiation therapy with a goal of palliation
is exemplified in this patient

00:18:09.429 --> 00:18:15.049
with a six- month history of difficulty in
swallowing solid foods, and recent weight loss.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:22.030
The esophagram demonstrated a mid-thoracic, obstructive lesion,

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:27.310
which on biopsy proved to be a squamous cell
carcinoma.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:33.560
Treatment will be given by rotating beam technique.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:41.920
On a contour of the patient's chest, radii are drawn from the axis of rotation to the
skins surface.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:48.900
The axis is located at the exact center of
the tumor. 

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:55.690
The radii will be used in the computation of the summation curves.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:03.180
These indicate a maximum dose region surrounding
the tumor,

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:07.070
and a much lower dose to the adjacent organs.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:16.240
The chances for cure in carcinoma of the esophagus
are statistically very small since metastases

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:19.400
are frequently present when the cancer is
diagnosed.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:26.400
Nevertheless, one can usually obtain significant
palliation with radiation therapy.

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:34.520
This split-screen comparison shows the grossly normal appearance of the esophagus,

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:40.840
approximately one month after the end of treatment.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:49.360
In addition to its use as a primary modality
of treatment, radiation therapy can be effective

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:52.540
in combination with other types of therapy.

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:58.620
For example, children with Wilms Tumor show
a higher survival rate when radiation therapy

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:03.660
is used, in addition to surgery and chemotherapy.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:09.340
In this post-operative patient, irradiation
may limit metastatic spread by destroying

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:15.169
any cancer cells which may have been left
behind in the renal bed.

00:20:15.169 --> 00:20:19.569
In some cases of this disease, preoperative
irradiation is given

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:26.740
 in an effort to destroy tumor cells which might be disseminated at nephrectomy.

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:36.419
In recent years, the combination of surgery,
radiation, and chemotherapy has resulted in

00:20:36.419 --> 00:20:41.639
a dramatic increase in survival rates in Wilm's
Tumor.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:53.539
Radiation therapy also has a major role in
the treatment of many other types of cancer.

00:20:53.539 --> 00:20:59.000
In a patient with carcinoma of the larynx,
with a lesion limited to one or both chords

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:06.040
and no fixation, radiation therapy can offer
a cure and has the advantage of preserving the voice.

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:15.080
This patient received a course of Cobalt-60
therapy over a period of 43 days.

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:22.450
One month after completion of treatment, laryngoscopy
showed no evidence of residual tumor.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:30.280
As with all patients treated by irradiation, this man is now seen regularly for follow-up examination.

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.350
[Physician:] Mr. Benkowsky, you feel well?

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:34.470
[Mr. Benkowsky:] Yes sir.

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:36.669
[Physician:] How long has it been now? About
five years since [inaudible] --

00:21:36.669 --> 00:21:39.039
[Mr. Benkowsky:] Five years -- five years,
longer than that...

00:21:39.039 --> 00:21:40.929
[Physician:] Mm-hmm. And your voice stays
strong?

00:21:40.929 --> 00:21:43.429
[Mr. Benkowsky:] Oh yes. Strong enough all
the time.

00:21:43.429 --> 00:21:44.809
[Physician:] And how about the swallowing?

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.710
[Mr. Benkowsky:] The swallowing is okay.

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:50.500
[Physician:] You want to take your dentures
out and put them into this?

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:56.310
[Mr. Benkowsky:] Oh sure, I'll do that. I
can do that.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:03.260
[Mr. Benkowsky:] Yes.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:08.700
[Physician:] Now open your mouth up wide.
Now stick your tongue out and let me see it.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:11.360
All right. Now say "E".

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:14.740
[Mr. Benkowsky:] "E".

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:18.120
[Mr. Benkowsky:] "E".

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:21.840
[Physician:] Say "E" again.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:26.000
[Mr. Benkowsky:] "E".

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.380
[Mr. Benkowsky:] "E" .

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:32.740
[Physician:] Very good. That's fine.

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:38.460
[Narrator:] Whether used with the intent to
cure, or with the intent to palliate...

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:43.900
alone, or in combination with other treatment modalities,

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:49.160
 radiation therapy has a major role in the management of cancer.

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:56.420
In fact, at some time in the course of treatment,
at least 50 percent of patients with cancer

00:22:56.500 --> 00:23:01.520
can benefit from radiation therapy.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:08.140
About half of these treated for cure, and
half for palliation.

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:15.140
[Radiation equipment is switched on and a
regular ticking/knocking sound is heard.]

00:23:15.660 --> 00:24:16.180
[Images of different types of cancers pre- and post-treatment are shown.]

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:22.840
[Images fade out; ticking stops.]

